   I don't. I agree with Kent --- there's a reason the specialized
key types were removed from the game, and it's because they eat up
inventory slots and add no challenge to the game. (Why not simply make
the skeleton keys three or four times rarer, and three or four times
heavier? It would do the same thing.)

   However, I'm neutral on the safes. They seem not out of character,
and they might provide some interesting interactions.

   Wands of opening should never work on safes; and "knock" only
when cast at very high skill levels. "The safe door jiggles."
Skeleton keys should work only rarely on safes; after all, they're
designed to prevent thieves from breaking in, so obviously they'd
resist skeleton keys!
   (I'd like to prevent skeleton keys from working /at all/ on safes,
but I couldn't figure out how to do that without reintroducing the
specialized key types again, or else making the safes /really/ hard
to get into.)

   Setting off a land mine under a safe should blow it open, destroying
the lock and perhaps scattering the contents.

   Safes should rust. (Should a rust monster be able to disintegrate
a safe by repeated attacks?)

   Speaking of which, disintegration blasts should obviously destroy
safes... although perhaps they'd destroy the contents, too. What's
the current behavior with chests and iceboxes?

   YAFM: Dropping a safe through a hole or down the stairs should 
occasionally generate a coyote corpse where the safe lands. (Free
food, if you can manage to lift a safe in the first place!)

